1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,560
There are many occasions when you would like to put a human in the loop to keep tabs

2
00:00:06,560 --> 00:00:08,839
on what an agent is doing.

3
00:00:08,839 --> 00:00:11,759
This is pretty easy to do with line graph.

4
00:00:11,759 --> 00:00:13,839
Let's see how this works.

5
00:00:13,839 --> 00:00:16,960
We're going to resume from where we left off in the last lesson.

6
00:00:16,960 --> 00:00:23,120
So let's start by setting up our environment variables.

7
00:00:23,120 --> 00:00:28,039
From there, we can make all the relevant imports and set up our checkpointer.

8
00:00:28,039 --> 00:00:34,600
We're now going to set up our agent state and we're going to make one small modification.

9
00:00:34,600 --> 00:00:40,960
In the previous example, we annotated the messages list with the operator dot add.

10
00:00:40,960 --> 00:00:44,560
That added messages to the existing messages array.

11
00:00:44,560 --> 00:00:51,599
However, for these human and the loop interactions, we may want to actually replace existing messages.

12
00:00:51,599 --> 00:00:57,079
In order to do that, we're going to write a custom reduced messages function that basically

13
00:00:57,079 --> 00:01:02,119
looks for messages with the same ID and if it sees that you're inserting one with the

14
00:01:02,119 --> 00:01:05,959
same ID as one that already exists, it's just going to replace that.

15
00:01:05,959 --> 00:01:08,679
Otherwise, it will append them.

16
00:01:08,679 --> 00:01:14,920
After that, we can create our same to Ville tool that we've been using and we can create

17
00:01:14,920 --> 00:01:16,719
our same agent.

18
00:01:16,719 --> 00:01:19,879
We're actually going to make one small modification.

19
00:01:19,879 --> 00:01:25,959
So when we compile the graph in addition to passing in the checkpointer, we're also going

20
00:01:25,960 --> 00:01:30,960
to pass this interrupts before equals action parameter.

21
00:01:30,960 --> 00:01:35,960
What this is going to do is it's going to add an interrupt before we call the action node.

22
00:01:35,960 --> 00:01:39,000
So the action node is where we call the tools.

23
00:01:39,000 --> 00:01:43,080
And so the reason that we're going to do this is we're going to add something that requires

24
00:01:43,080 --> 00:01:46,840
manual approval before we run any tools.

25
00:01:46,840 --> 00:01:54,720
This is useful when you want to make sure that tools are executed correctly.

26
00:01:54,719 --> 00:02:00,480
This interrupt happens before we call the action node where all tools are called.

27
00:02:00,480 --> 00:02:04,280
Sometimes, you may only want to interrupt if a certain tool is called.

28
00:02:04,280 --> 00:02:12,120
That's covered in other parts of the documentation and I encourage you to check that out later.

29
00:02:12,120 --> 00:02:16,159
Let's initialize the agent with the same system prompt and model and checkpointer that we've

30
00:02:16,159 --> 00:02:19,000
been using.

31
00:02:19,000 --> 00:02:24,079
We now call it and we'll pass in this thread config using the thread ID of one because

32
00:02:24,080 --> 00:02:28,320
this is a separate notebook, it'll start from fresh.

33
00:02:28,320 --> 00:02:33,120
We stream back responses and we stop after this AI message.

34
00:02:33,120 --> 00:02:36,720
This is because this AI message is saying that we should call a tool.

35
00:02:36,720 --> 00:02:41,800
But we have that interrupt before parameter, which stops it there.

36
00:02:41,800 --> 00:02:46,760
One thing we can do from here is get the current state of the graph for this thread.

37
00:02:46,760 --> 00:02:51,400
So in order to do that, we'll pass in this thread config.

38
00:02:51,400 --> 00:02:55,680
We'll get back this configuration object, which has a few parameters.

39
00:02:55,680 --> 00:02:58,879
We can see that the largest ones are this list of messages here.

40
00:02:58,879 --> 00:03:04,879
This is the state of the graph at this point in time.

41
00:03:04,879 --> 00:03:07,560
We can also see that it has a next parameter.

42
00:03:07,560 --> 00:03:09,960
This is the node that is to be called next.

43
00:03:09,960 --> 00:03:11,719
We can see here that it's action.

44
00:03:11,719 --> 00:03:16,840
This means that we're about to call the action node.

45
00:03:16,840 --> 00:03:22,319
In order to continue, we can call stream again with the same thread config and just pass

46
00:03:22,319 --> 00:03:26,719
in none as the input.

47
00:03:26,719 --> 00:03:28,640
This will stream back results.

48
00:03:28,640 --> 00:03:34,400
And we will see that we get the tool message from calling the tool.

49
00:03:34,400 --> 00:03:38,120
And then we also get the final AI message.

50
00:03:38,120 --> 00:03:43,159
Notice that there was no break in between the action node and the LLM node because we didn't

51
00:03:43,159 --> 00:03:47,359
add any interrupt there.

52
00:03:47,359 --> 00:03:57,280
If we now get the state, we can see that the messages list contains the full list of messages.

53
00:03:57,280 --> 00:04:00,120
And if we get the next parameter, we can see that it's empty.

54
00:04:00,120 --> 00:04:03,439
There's nothing left to be done.

55
00:04:03,439 --> 00:04:08,519
For fun, we can write some code that runs this in a little loop and prompts us for input

56
00:04:08,519 --> 00:04:10,680
about whether to continue or not.

57
00:04:10,680 --> 00:04:15,800
We'll pass in a new thread ID so we start afresh.

58
00:04:15,800 --> 00:04:20,959
We get this little input box here asking us whether we want to proceed, we can hit yes,

59
00:04:20,959 --> 00:04:24,280
and then the agent continues on its way.

60
00:04:24,280 --> 00:04:28,560
This is a good time to stop and try it out with other inputs, try adding different

61
00:04:28,560 --> 00:04:33,240
places to interrupt before and see what happens.

62
00:04:33,240 --> 00:04:38,319
Before we get to the next section, let's talk a little bit more about state memory.

63
00:04:38,319 --> 00:04:44,599
As a graph is executing, a snapshot of each state is stored in memory.

64
00:04:44,599 --> 00:04:46,120
What's in that snapshot?

65
00:04:46,120 --> 00:04:51,040
Well, there's the agent state, which you've already defined, and then there's some other

66
00:04:51,040 --> 00:04:52,439
useful things.

67
00:04:52,439 --> 00:04:58,120
For example, there's the thread and a unique identifier for each of the snapshots.

68
00:04:58,120 --> 00:05:00,279
That's the thread TS right there.

69
00:05:00,279 --> 00:05:03,959
You can use that to access the snapshots.

70
00:05:03,959 --> 00:05:06,759
There's some commands to access memory.

71
00:05:06,759 --> 00:05:09,599
There's get state, which you've already seen.

72
00:05:09,599 --> 00:05:15,360
If you provide the thread without the unique identifier and just return that thread ID,

73
00:05:15,360 --> 00:05:17,759
it will return the current state.

74
00:05:17,759 --> 00:05:24,920
There's also get state history, which returns an iterator over all of the state snapshots.

75
00:05:24,920 --> 00:05:30,240
You can use the iterator to get access to all of the unique identifiers for each of the

76
00:05:30,240 --> 00:05:31,240
states.

77
00:05:31,240 --> 00:05:32,240
What can you do with that?

78
00:05:32,240 --> 00:05:34,480
Well, here's an example.

79
00:05:34,480 --> 00:05:40,160
In the thread identifier or rather that thread TS, the unique identifier, you could, for

80
00:05:40,160 --> 00:05:46,200
example, access that first state, state one, and use that in an invoke command.

81
00:05:46,200 --> 00:05:51,600
That'll use state one as the current state or the starting point for the rest of the graph.

82
00:05:51,600 --> 00:05:54,040
This is effectively time travel.

83
00:05:54,040 --> 00:05:58,680
Conversely, without that thread TS, if you just pass in the thread ID, it will use

84
00:05:58,680 --> 00:06:01,840
the current state of the thread as the starting point.

85
00:06:01,840 --> 00:06:07,840
You can also use that unique identifier to access a particular state.

86
00:06:07,840 --> 00:06:13,880
You can then modify that state, and then you can use update state to update the state

87
00:06:13,880 --> 00:06:18,520
and store it back into memory in the location of the current state.

88
00:06:18,520 --> 00:06:24,760
From there, if you run stream or invoke, it'll use the new state, the modified state,

89
00:06:24,760 --> 00:06:26,080
as its starting point.

90
00:06:26,080 --> 00:06:30,560
All right, you'll be seeing some examples of these in the upcoming section, so let's get

91
00:06:30,560 --> 00:06:32,879
back to it.

92
00:06:32,879 --> 00:06:36,280
Now let's show an example of modifying the state.

93
00:06:36,280 --> 00:06:44,959
So let's start a new thread, and let's ask it, what's the weather in LA?

94
00:06:44,959 --> 00:06:50,319
At this point in the thread, we have two messages, the human message and then the AI message,

95
00:06:50,319 --> 00:06:54,639
which is saying to search Tivoli for current weather in Los Angeles.

96
00:06:54,639 --> 00:06:55,639
But let's modify this.

97
00:06:55,639 --> 00:07:01,800
Let's pretend we were instead asking about the weather in Louisiana, Nala, Los Angeles.

98
00:07:01,800 --> 00:07:06,919
So how would we modify this to correct the agent action?

99
00:07:06,919 --> 00:07:17,159
First, let's save the current state of the graph to a variable called current values.

100
00:07:17,159 --> 00:07:22,879
The last message we have in this state is the AI message, which is saying to search for

101
00:07:22,879 --> 00:07:31,079
a particular search term, in this case, current weather in Los Angeles.

102
00:07:31,079 --> 00:07:38,759
We can drill in even further into see the list of tool calls associated with this message.

103
00:07:38,759 --> 00:07:41,279
Let's now update these tool calls.

104
00:07:41,279 --> 00:07:45,839
In order to do that, we can first get the ID associated with the tool call.

105
00:07:45,839 --> 00:07:50,040
We'll then update the tool calls property to be a list.

106
00:07:50,040 --> 00:07:51,640
This list will have one element.

107
00:07:51,640 --> 00:07:53,000
It's a dictionary.

108
00:07:53,000 --> 00:07:54,000
It's got one tool call.

109
00:07:54,000 --> 00:07:56,280
It's calling the Tivoli search results JSON.

110
00:07:56,280 --> 00:07:58,000
That's the same as before.

111
00:07:58,000 --> 00:07:59,800
But the arguments are different this time.

112
00:07:59,800 --> 00:08:04,400
We have query with current weather in Louisiana.

113
00:08:04,400 --> 00:08:08,879
This doesn't actually do anything until we call update state on the graph.

114
00:08:08,879 --> 00:08:13,240
We're going to pass in the thread to config, and this is so we know which thread we're operating

115
00:08:13,240 --> 00:08:14,240
on.

116
00:08:14,240 --> 00:08:21,840
We're going to pass in the new values that we want to override with.

117
00:08:21,840 --> 00:08:26,040
If we get the current state of the graph now, we can see that we have this search term

118
00:08:26,040 --> 00:08:34,039
here, current weather in Louisiana.

119
00:08:34,039 --> 00:08:38,799
If we continue from here, we can see that it calls Tivoli with the current weather in Louisiana

120
00:08:38,799 --> 00:08:42,159
and gets back some response, and then responds accordingly.

121
00:08:42,159 --> 00:08:45,719
The current weather in Louisiana shows sunny conditions.

122
00:08:45,719 --> 00:08:50,279
We've now shown how we can modify the state of the graph in order to control what the

123
00:08:50,279 --> 00:08:51,480
agent does.

124
00:08:51,480 --> 00:08:57,360
One important thing to note is that we're actually keeping a running list of all these states.

125
00:08:57,360 --> 00:09:02,679
When we modified the state, we actually created a new state, and that became the new state,

126
00:09:02,679 --> 00:09:07,360
and then every time we update it with results from the nodes, it's actually creating a new

127
00:09:07,360 --> 00:09:09,360
state one after the other.

128
00:09:09,360 --> 00:09:14,800
This is really nice because it actually allows us to go back and visit previous states

129
00:09:14,800 --> 00:09:18,800
in something we're calling time travel.

130
00:09:18,800 --> 00:09:24,440
In order to do this, we can call get state history on the graph, passing in again the

131
00:09:24,440 --> 00:09:25,440
thread ID.

132
00:09:25,440 --> 00:09:35,600
We'll then start building up this list of states over time.

133
00:09:35,600 --> 00:09:41,320
If we get the last state in this list, we can see that it's actually the earliest one.

134
00:09:41,320 --> 00:09:45,040
This is the one where it was looking up the current weather in Los Angeles.

135
00:09:45,040 --> 00:09:50,840
This was the original state update that was made based on the first language model call.

136
00:09:50,840 --> 00:09:55,480
If we wanted to go back in time and resume from this checkpoint where it was looking for

137
00:09:55,480 --> 00:09:58,600
Los Angeles, we easily can.

138
00:09:58,600 --> 00:10:05,160
In order to do that, all we have to do is call graph.stream again, pass and none, and now

139
00:10:05,240 --> 00:10:09,399
notice that we're passing in to replay.config.

140
00:10:09,399 --> 00:10:14,799
So to replay is the state where we want to resume from, and config is just the configuration

141
00:10:14,799 --> 00:10:18,439
parameters that tell us that we're resuming from this state.

142
00:10:18,439 --> 00:10:22,959
So if we run this, we'll start to see that it's searching for the current weather in Los

143
00:10:22,959 --> 00:10:23,959
Angeles.

144
00:10:23,959 --> 00:10:28,959
It's getting a result back from Tavilly, and then it's generating a response, and that's

145
00:10:28,959 --> 00:10:31,279
our final answer.

146
00:10:31,279 --> 00:10:36,600
Something we can also do is go back in time, and then edit it from there.

147
00:10:36,600 --> 00:10:40,860
So here, we have this two replay config, and this is where it's the current weather in

148
00:10:40,860 --> 00:10:42,600
Los Angeles.

149
00:10:42,600 --> 00:10:51,199
So we can do the same thing that we did before where we modify this state.

150
00:10:51,199 --> 00:10:56,159
And so we'll modify this to do current weather in LA, acu weather, presuming that we want

151
00:10:56,159 --> 00:10:57,919
to response from acu weather.

152
00:10:57,919 --> 00:11:02,679
The reason this is different than before is that here, we're going back in time, and

153
00:11:02,679 --> 00:11:09,360
then editing as opposed to before where we were editing from the most recent state.

154
00:11:09,360 --> 00:11:15,039
We can update the state of this two replay, and we'll get back this branch state where

155
00:11:15,039 --> 00:11:21,360
we've branched off with this modification.

156
00:11:21,360 --> 00:11:26,159
If we now call graph.stream with none on this branch state, we can see that we're looking

157
00:11:26,159 --> 00:11:30,719
up acu weather, and getting a result from there, and we're getting back a new answer,

158
00:11:30,719 --> 00:11:36,360
and then we're responding from the AI.

159
00:11:36,360 --> 00:11:41,719
Another thing we can do is add messages to a state at any given point in time.

160
00:11:41,719 --> 00:11:46,000
So here, we have this two replay config, which we've modified to have the current weather

161
00:11:46,000 --> 00:11:48,240
in LA, acu weather.

162
00:11:48,240 --> 00:11:54,120
Now let's presume that instead of actually calling Tavilly, we wanted to mock out a response.

163
00:11:54,120 --> 00:11:58,759
We can do that by appending a new message into the state.

164
00:11:58,759 --> 00:12:05,639
We're going to grab the ID of the tool call that we're supposed to be making, and we're

165
00:12:05,639 --> 00:12:10,399
going to create this state update, which is a list of messages, and it's a new message

166
00:12:10,399 --> 00:12:11,399
this time.

167
00:12:11,399 --> 00:12:13,159
It's got a tool call ID of this.

168
00:12:13,159 --> 00:12:17,919
It's got a name of Tavilly search, and it's got a content of 54 degrees Celsius.

169
00:12:17,919 --> 00:12:20,000
So here, this is a new message.

170
00:12:20,000 --> 00:12:24,960
So when we update the state in the graph, it's not going to replace an existing message,

171
00:12:24,960 --> 00:12:28,759
it's going to append it to the list of messages.

172
00:12:28,759 --> 00:12:31,120
We're now going to update the state of the graph.

173
00:12:31,120 --> 00:12:35,960
But because we are actually adding and pretending that an action has taken place, as opposed

174
00:12:35,960 --> 00:12:40,480
to modifying the existing state, we need to do an additional thing.

175
00:12:40,480 --> 00:12:45,240
We need to add this as node equals action parameter here.

176
00:12:45,240 --> 00:12:50,360
What this is basically doing is it's saying that the state update that we're making isn't

177
00:12:50,360 --> 00:12:51,960
just a modification.

178
00:12:51,960 --> 00:12:55,919
We're actually making this update as if we were the action node.

179
00:12:55,919 --> 00:13:00,399
The reason that this is relevant is because before we add this message, the current state

180
00:13:00,399 --> 00:13:03,720
of the graph is about to go into the action node.

181
00:13:03,720 --> 00:13:08,159
But after we add this message, we don't want it to go into the action mode anymore.

182
00:13:08,159 --> 00:13:12,919
So we're basically saying that when we update the state, we're acting as if we were the

183
00:13:12,920 --> 00:13:16,000
action node.

184
00:13:16,000 --> 00:13:21,760
If we now call stream on this new configuration, we can see that it doesn't take an action

185
00:13:21,760 --> 00:13:22,760
anymore.

186
00:13:22,760 --> 00:13:26,640
Rather, it just calls the model and responds with this AI message.

187
00:13:26,640 --> 00:13:29,600
The current weather in Los Angeles is 54 degrees Celsius.

188
00:13:29,600 --> 00:13:33,320
This is what we pretended that the tool had responded with.

189
00:13:33,320 --> 00:13:38,200
This has shown off a lot of really advanced and complicated human in the loop interaction

190
00:13:38,200 --> 00:13:39,200
patterns.

191
00:13:39,280 --> 00:13:43,240
So you've learned how to add a break before a node takes place.

192
00:13:43,240 --> 00:13:48,280
This allows for humans to approve or deny specific actions.

193
00:13:48,280 --> 00:13:53,920
You've also shown how you can go back in time and how you can modify the state either the

194
00:13:53,920 --> 00:13:56,759
current state or the past state.

195
00:13:56,759 --> 00:14:01,680
Additionally, you've also shown how you can update the state manually.

196
00:14:01,680 --> 00:14:06,879
This allows you to manually give the agent the result of calling a tool rather than actually

197
00:14:06,879 --> 00:14:08,759
calling a tool itself.

198
00:14:08,759 --> 00:14:13,500
All of these human in the loop patterns better serve to facilitate how you interact with

199
00:14:13,500 --> 00:14:14,500
agents.

200
00:14:14,500 --> 00:14:17,200
They give you more control over what it's doing.

201
00:14:17,200 --> 00:14:21,879
It allows you to go back to a previous point in time, and by editing, it allows you to

202
00:14:21,879 --> 00:14:24,840
correct what it has done.

203
00:14:24,840 --> 00:14:27,279
So far, we've been working with a pretty simple agent.

204
00:14:27,279 --> 00:14:31,480
It's got one LLM call, one prompt, and it's got a pretty simple state, just a list

205
00:14:31,480 --> 00:14:32,759
of messages.

206
00:14:32,759 --> 00:14:37,360
And the next and final example in this course, we're going to create a much more complicated

207
00:14:37,360 --> 00:14:42,360
agent, made up of multiple LLM calls that has a pretty complex state.

208
00:14:42,360 --> 00:14:42,800
See you there.
